    "description": "This is funding phase 2 of this project. An email was recently sent giving short videos to see the good results phase 1 produced. \nThey reached out again wanting to start the second phase of this project. I acknowledged how what they have shared about the good harvest of the first phase really filled my heart. I can see it is energizing the full community. It was agreed that they should use the next funds to continue to develop these first 12 farms so that the model they are developing can be sustainable and then used to help the 136 Agrarian communities. What was agreed upon was that we would send $8000 to do that. Last Friday on August 22nd they received these funds. Again, this is a collaboration with the Fountain helping to organize, work closely with the youth and elders and keep good accounting on all; KINS Flow Funds($4000); and the International Flow Funds of Chris Lindstrom($4000).\n\nThe request for this next phase and what it will focus on is in this partial email below.\n\nA letter from those overseeing the food sovereignty program. In that letter what they share is:\nOf course, we know yam as first crop and the beginning of first harvest season in the land and this year came bountiful so sound. Nweke Ezenwanyi is the beginning of second planting season in Ezza tradition. We all are abreast of the fact that the Ezza People though Internally displaced are that feeding the Nigeria Nation even as peasants.\n\nSo following the success of the twenty-four farms first stage of food sovereignty project of Yusuf Offia Foundation to enschew abject poverty, perpetual penury, hunger, starvation and bad political leadership but reclaim our land. The Fountain Earth sponsored gardens are progressive yieldful progressive. And now we are poised For the second planting season that is supposed supposseful to span till November 4th 2025.\n\nIt will involve irrigation system with animal husbandry and to be replicated to reflect the 136 agrarian Communities of the Ezza People of Nigeria in trios reflective of the Elders, women and youth.",
