    "amountNumeric": 1509.39,
    "description": "We gave funds to Maybloom because of their work in building a community education center and demonstration site for people to learn about permaculture, gardening, self care, and connect with nature. Their impact reports are beautiful stories and you can read them at https://bloomnetwork.earth/eugene. \n\nThey, like many communities, we discovered through this process, are building infrastructure. For example, they built a greenhouse and irrigation system. They worked with bees to help them overcome the Varroa mite, composted food scraps to prevent them from becoming methane, created more than 70 gallons of biochar and donated biochar to other community members, and learned about ethical and sustainable practices by deciding whether or not to harvest honey.\n\nWith this group and some other sub-projects in other groups we funded, there has been interesting discussion about rewarding work done on private properties. Some people disagree, but most folks feel that reforestation, carbon sequestration, and community healing needs to happen everywhere whether it's on private or community-owned land. The workers and land-owners are part of the community and the learnings and any financial flows involved circulate in the community. \n\nMaybloom is also a community education center so it's not in a questionable zone really, but the question came up moreso in Diamante Bridge Collective among their community members, since they are a larger, decentralized community and many of their works are in public areas such as the sides of roads, the local soccer field, and a community land trust owned by their cooperative association. However, there are more private communities in their area where workers are paid to do reforestation and agroforestry installation work. Those workers also contribute to community projects in shared spaces. Generally, we've been leaning toward - 1) when people are paid by market forces for this kind of work they're usually not paid very much, and 2) we want to encourage people to continue doing the work that is needed for Mother Earth and all her creatures to be well, and if they end up making good money doing that, that's a great outcome. As long as folks are sharing their learnings in their impact reports, we're happy they are sharing knowledge to the global commons.",
    "recipientNotified": "Yes. They've asked for their video to not be shared, for privacy, but their impact reports are viewable.",
    "learnings": "About bees!! Maybloom's reports on bloom about their beekeeping are insightful, moving, sad, and inspiring as they share their journey on caring for bees in a time when bees have a hard time surviving.",
