    "id": "broadfield-enterprises-uganda",
    "timestamp": "10/17/2025 19:38:54",
    "funderName": "Rachel Steinbeisser",
    "recipientName": "Broadfield Enterprises, Uganda",
    "location": "Jinja, Uganda",
    "disbursementDate": "2/3/2025",
    "amount": "1,158.52",
    "amountNumeric": 1158.52,
    "description": "We gave funds to this group because of their permaculture agroforestry economic empowerment and climate healing work across 5,000 farmers and over 100,000 school children in Uganda. \n\nOne of their impacts is scaling a Permaculture School Enterprise model of self funding. It took them 20 years to get permaculture curriculum approved across the entire Ugandan school system. They teach kids permaculture, the kids then plant organic permaculture gardens at home and their families have fresh healthy food to eat. One of the things the kids learn to plant at school is coffee. The coffee is sold through the cooperatives network and the profits from those sales go directly back to the schools!!\n\nThey also transform degraded, poor soils that are red barren fields into thriving permaculture arboretums, by planting 500 indigenous plant species among 4 export crops. This repairs local microclimates, prevents flooding risk, greatly increases biodiversity, provides beautiful places for people to be rather than segregated gardens, they are living food forests providing income, education, and food sovereignty for Africans. \n\nYou can read about their work at https://bloomnetwork.earth/uganda-jinja and on their website at https://broadfieldpermaculture.org/",
    "recipientNotified": "yes",
    "learnings": "A surprising outcome of us giving funds to this group is that they have since opened a distribution and fulfillment center in Germany and also in the US. They want Bloom Network to invest in those entities as part-owners, so a share of profits can go into our Local Action Rewards pool for flowing funds to communities around the world! We are working on finding a philanthropic funder to support this in happening. \n\nWe also learned about the volume of coffee and finances they are running through their community, and that they have increased farmers income by 30-50%! Through a combination of: Higher yields due to soil regeneration and diversified cropping.\n\nPremium prices for certified organic and direct-trade produce (vanilla, coffee, dried fruits).\n\nReduced input costs since permaculture relies zero  on external fertilizers and chemicals.\n\nMarket access through our trade partnerships, which smallholders often lack on their own.\n\nThis combination ensures both immediate income gains and long-term stability for our farmers.\n\nIncluding maximizing all forms of soil to offer alternative arable soils.",
    "coordinates": {
      "lat": 0.5751567,
      "lng": 33.2805299
    }
