    "id": "department-of-bioregion-jay-conrad-seattle-salish-",
    "timestamp": "10/17/2025 20:17:04",
    "funderName": "Brandon Letsinger-Brown",
    "recipientName": "Department of Bioregion. Jay Conrad. Seattle, Salish Sea.",
    "location": "Seattle, Salish Sea.",
    "disbursementDate": "4/30/2025",
    "amount": "1500",
    "amountNumeric": 1500,
    "description": "We chose to give $1,500 to Jay T. Conrad in response to an emergency request to support the relocation and rehousing of trans and nonbinary people escaping persecution in U.S. states that have enacted anti-trans legislation and policies. Jay is a deeply respected nonbinary advocate, legal scholar, and community organizer who has devoted their professional and personal life to advancing privacy, safety, and human rights for LGBTQIA2S+ communities. Their leadership—spanning law, policy, and direct mutual aid—bridges both systemic change and hands-on care for individuals in crisis.\n\nThis flow fund was intended to provide immediate relief for those being displaced, while also strengthening a longer-term network of safety and belonging within the Cascadia bioregion. Together, we hope to demonstrate how a bioregional approach to regeneration includes creating places of refuge—where diversity is celebrated, communities look after one another, and those most vulnerable can live freely and safely as themselves.",
    "recipientNotified": "Yes.",
    "learnings": "Through this experience, I learned how urgently flexible, trust-based funding mechanisms are needed to protect the safety and dignity of marginalized people facing systemic persecution. The $1,500 emergency flow fund to Jay Conrad supported their efforts to rehouse and relocate trans and nonbinary individuals fleeing states where anti-trans legislation and violence have made it unsafe to live. The experience underscored how Cascadia can and must serve as a refuge—a bioregion that embodies safety, inclusion, and mutual aid for those displaced by oppressive policies elsewhere.\n\nThis flow reminded me that regeneration isn’t only about ecosystems—it’s also about social and cultural resilience. Helping to resource trusted community leaders like Jay, who are already deeply embedded in advocacy and mutual support networks, demonstrated the power of acting quickly, relationally, and with care. It reaffirmed that bioregionalism must include protecting those who are being uprooted, and that sometimes the most regenerative act is simply helping someone find safety and belonging in a new home.",
    "coordinates": {
      "lat": 47.6058329,
      "lng": -122.3405816
    }
